        "Above all, 'Ata (RA) left a legacy of social empowerment. He proved that under the shade of Islam, a former slave of African descent could become the teacher of the Arab aristocracy. His life story empowered generations of non-Arab Muslims (Mawali) to pursue scholarship, fundamentally shaping the intellectual diversity of the Islamic Golden Age. He died having performed Hajj over 70 times, leaving behind a world where his name was synonymous with the Sacred Sanctuary.",
