        "In his later years, specifically during the caliphate of Hisham ibn Abd al-Malik, he served as a tutor to the princes while continuing his scholarly output. Despite the political turmoil of the Umayyad-Abbasid transition era, he remained a neutral, stabilizing force for Islamic law. He eventually retired to his estate in Shaghb, located in the northern Hijaz. Even in retirement, he was surrounded by piles of books, which his wife famously remarked were 'harsher than three rival wives' because of the time they took from him. He passed away on the 17th of Ramadan, 124 AH, leaving behind a clarified path for all future Muhaddithin.",
